review_comment:
I was wondering why the first overload here does not cover this but then I remember now that Mapping is not covariant in the key type:
https://github.com/python/typing/issues/445

For now the overload you have added here is fine but in future this could lead to an explosion of overloads. We probably need to define a covariant version of Mapping. Looking at the implementation of Basic.subs the type could be like:
```python
class SupportsItems(Protocol[K, V]):
    def items(self) -> Iterable[tuple[K, V]]:
        ...

@overload
def subs(
    self,
    arg1: Iterable[tuple[Basic | complex, Basic | complex]]
    | SupportsItems[Basic | complex, Basic | complex],
    arg2: None = None,
    **kwargs: Any,
) -> Basic: ...
@overload
def subs(
    self, arg1: Basic | complex, arg2: Basic | complex, **kwargs: Any
) -> Basic: ...
```
