review_comment:
This is really not the right way to handle this sort of situation. Branch on the condition early:
```python
        ring = self.ring

        if isinstance(fv, PolyElement):
            if not self:
                return (ring.zero, ring.zero)
            elif not fv:
                raise ZeroDivisionError("polynomial division")
            elif fv.ring != ring:
                raise ValueError("self and f must have the same ring")
            else:
                return self._div(fv)
        else:
            if not self:
                return ([], ring.zero)
            elif not any(fv):
                raise ZeroDivisionError("polynomial division")
            elif not all(f.ring == ring for f in fv):
                raise ValueError("self and f must have the same ring")
            else:
                return self._div_list(fv)
```
It is much better to branch early and then have code where all the types are known than to have a conditional expression at every line. Of course now having branched you can see clearly where the code could be moved into other functions.

I'm not sure if it would break anything but this should really be checking the ring first before checking `if not self` and `if not fv`. It should also check for division by zero before checking whether self is zero.

This is duplicating everything from `_rem_list`. Is there some advantage here in terms of efficiency compared to just doing this the obvious way:
```
def _rem(self, g):
     return self._rem_list([g])
```

