review_comment:
I was thinking initially that we can probably restructure `__mul__` exactly the way you did `__add__`:

```python
@overload
def __mul__(self, other: PolyElement[Er] | Er | int, /) -> PolyElement[Er]: ...


@overload
def __mul__(
    self, other: PolyElement[PolyElement[Er]], /
) -> PolyElement[PolyElement[Er]]: ...


def __mul__(
    self, other: PolyElement[Er] | Er | int | PolyElement[PolyElement[Er]], /
) -> PolyElement[Er] | PolyElement[PolyElement[Er]]:
    if not self or not other:
        return self.ring.zero

    if self.ring.is_element(other):
        return self._mul(other)

    res = self._try_mul_ground(other)
    if res is not NotImplemented:
        return res

    if isinstance(other, PolyElement):
        return other._try_mul_ground(self)

    return NotImplemented


def __rmul__(self, other: Er | int) -> PolyElement[Er]:
    if not self or not other:
        return self.ring.zero

    return self._try_mul_ground(other)


def _try_mul_ground(self, other: object) -> PolyElement[Er] | NotImplementedType:
    ring = self.ring

    domain = ring.domain
    if domain.of_type(other):
        return self._mul_ground(other)

    try:
        cp2 = ring.domain_new(other)
    except CoercionFailed:
        return NotImplemented
    else:
        return self._mul_ground(cp2)
```
But this doesn't work, it was failing `test_tan` in the `ring_series` tests.  The issue, I think,  was in the handling of nested polynomial ring. Originally `__mul__` does this:

```python
elif (
    isinstance(ring.domain, PolynomialRing) and ring.domain.ring == other.ring
):
    return self.mul_ground(other)  # directly calls mul_ground with the PolyElement
```

But in the restructured version I have shown above we have condensed this to:
```python
res = self._try_mul_ground(other) # I think this goes through domain_new conversion first
```

I think this conversion messes things up. 

Although, this version works:

```python

# overloads here

def __mul__(
    self, other: PolyElement[Er] | Er | int | PolyElement[PolyElement[Er]], /
) -> PolyElement[Er] | PolyElement[PolyElement[Er]]:

    if not self or not other:
        return self.ring.zero

    if self.ring.is_element(other):
        return self._mul(other)

    if isinstance(other, PolyElement):
        ring = self.ring
        if isinstance(ring.domain, PolynomialRing) and ring.domain.ring == other.ring:
            return self._mul_ground(other)
        elif (
            isinstance(other.ring.domain, PolynomialRing)
            and other.ring.domain.ring == ring
        ):
            return other._mul_ground(self)

    res = self._try_mul_ground(other)
    if res is not NotImplemented:
        return res

    if isinstance(other, PolyElement):
        return other._try_mul_ground(self)

    return NotImplemented

# rest of the restructured code is same
```
This doesn't fail but I'm wondering if this working restructured `__mul__` worthwhile keeping or not since partially it still keeps that awkward logic of checking nested rings and stuff.

