review_comment:
This function should be typed as
```python
@overload
def _power_series_ring(K: Field[Ef], prec: int = 6) -> TSeriesRingField[Ef]:
    ...
@overload
def _power_series_ring(K: Domain[Er], prec: int = 6) -> TSeriesRing[Er]:
    ...

def _power_series_ring(K: Domain[Er] | Field[Ef], prec: int = 6) -> TSeriesRing[Er] | TSeriesRingField[Ef]:
    """
    Helper function for the Power Series Ring classes to create a base ring from lower
    power series rings.
    """
    if K.is_ZZ:
        R_ZZ: TSeriesRing[MPZ] = PowerSeriesRingZZ(prec)
        return cast("TSeriesRing[Er]", R_ZZ)
    elif K.is_QQ:
        R_QQ: TSeriesRingField[MPQ] = PowerSeriesRingQQ(prec)
        return cast("TSeriesRingField[Ef]", R_QQ)
    else:
        raise ValueError(f"Unsupported ground domain: {K}")
```

Using `type[Any]` here disables all of the type checking. You could write `FlintPowerSeriesRingZZ = str` and type checkers would accept it. This is why we need to avoid using `Any` whenever possible.

I think there is only one place where `Any` is actually necessary which is the `Any` in `PowerSeriesRing[Any, Er]`. To keep that Any in one place only it is probably best to make an alias in `base.py` like:
```python
class _PowerSeriesRing[Ts, Er]:
    ...

PowerSeriesRing: TypeAlias = _PowerSeriesRing[Any, Er]
```
Then  I think it should not be necessary to use `Any` anywhere else.

Then here you could do:
```python
from sympy.polys.series import PowerSeriesRing

_PowerSeriesRingZZ: Callable[[int], PowerSeriesRing[MPZ]]
_PowerSeriesRingQQ: Callable[[int], PowerSeriesRing[MPQ]]

if GROUND_TYPES == "flint":
    # Make use of flint implementation if flint available.
    from sympy.polys.series.ringflint import (
        FlintPowerSeriesRingZZ as _PowerSeriesRingZZ,
        FlintPowerSeriesRingQQ as _PowerSeriesRingQQ,
    )
else:
    # Fall back to python implementation.
    from sympy.polys.series.ringpython import (
        PythonPowerSeriesRingZZ as _PowerSeriesRingZZ,
        PythonPowerSeriesRingQQ as _PowerSeriesRingQQ,
    )

def power_series_ring(domain: Domain[Er], prec: int = 6) -> PowerSeriesRing[Er]:
    if domain.is_ZZ:
        return cast(PowerSeriesRing[Er], _PowerSeriesRingZZ(prec))
    elif domain.is_QQ:
        return cast(PowerSeriesRing[Er], _PowerSeriesRingQQ(prec))
    else:
        raise TypeError("Ground domain must be an instance of QQ or ZZ")
```

There may be a misunderstanding, so let me explain in detail. In `sqrt_mod`, three `@overload`s are declared:

```python
@overload
def sqrt_mod(
    a: SupportsIndex, p: SupportsIndex, all_roots: Literal[False] = False
) -> int | None: ...
@overload
def sqrt_mod(
    a: SupportsIndex, p: SupportsIndex, all_roots: Literal[True]
) -> list[int]: ...
@overload
def sqrt_mod(
    a: SupportsIndex, p: SupportsIndex, all_roots: bool,
) -> int | list[int] | None: ...
```

On the other hand, `nthroot_mod` only declares two:

```python
@overload
def nthroot_mod(
    a: SupportsIndex,
    n: SupportsIndex,
    p: SupportsIndex,
    all_roots: Literal[False] = False,
) -> int | None: ...
@overload
def nthroot_mod(
    a: SupportsIndex, n: SupportsIndex, p: SupportsIndex, all_roots: Literal[True]
) -> list[int]: ...
```

Since both functions have the property that the return type changes depending on `all_roots`, I expected the `@overload` declarations to be made in the same way. In that case, it seems that either the extra overload in `sqrt_mod` is unnecessary, or the `nthroot_mod` declarations are insufficient.

