This line is long. There is no need for it to be long though:
```python
if simplified != integrand:
    steps = integral_steps(simplified, symbol)
    return RewriteRule(integrand, symbol, simplified, steps)
```

Is this version of the reversion algorithm always more efficient than the previous one? Have you done any timings?

I would not be surprised if this implementation is slower for small `n`. It can only be asymptotically faster in the regime where Karatsuba kicks in which means you need a good size `n`.

I think it would be good to keep both versions and to have something that chooses which one to use e.g.:
```
def dup_series_reversion(...):
    if len(f) > 100:
        return _dup_series_lagrange
   else:
        return ...
```

