review_comment:
@TiloRC @oscarbenjamin 
I have removed the unnecessary portions and have added this code proposal:
```python
@PrimePredicate.register(Pow)
def _(expr, assumptions):
    """
    Integer**Integer     -> !Prime
    """
    if expr.is_number:
        return _PrimePredicate_number(expr, assumptions)
    if ask(Q.integer(expr.exp), assumptions) and \
            ask(Q.integer(expr.base), assumptions):
        if ask(Q.prime(expr.base), assumptions)==False or expr.exp.is_number==True:
            return False
    return None
```
This essentially marks false for those cases of exponents(x^y) where either the exponent is a number given the fact if the number is 1 it would just not call the pow handler as it will be evaluated as x and thus any other power would not make it a prime(given the base is an integer), and secondly if the base of the exponent is not a prime then it will surely evaluate to not prime in case the exponent y is an integer. In all other cases it should return None due to remaining ambiguity of either the base or the exponent.
I have also added additional regression tests for the pow handler such as checking for cases where the base is a number and the exponent is symbolic(that is ambiguous) and the extreme case when both base and exponent are symbols standing for any integer. 
Please let me know if there are any other issues! Thanks.

@TiloRC 
I think I understand your question but I think the only case where we can certainly conclude that the expression is going to be prime that is return ```True``` is when we are sure that the base of the exponent is prime and the power is one and if the power is 1 like if it is a number then the Pow handler will not be called and just to include the case where something like ask(Q.prime(x**y),Q.prime(x) & Q.eq(y,1)) doesn't make that much sense. But if this is the case we want to also account for with efficient calls(avoiding repeated calls)..then is the below code proposal suitable?
```
@PrimePredicate.register(Pow)
def _(expr, assumptions):
    """
    Integer**Integer -> !Prime
    """
    if expr.is_number:
        return _PrimePredicate_number(expr, assumptions)
    if ask(Q.integer(expr.exp), assumptions) and ask(Q.integer(expr.base), assumptions):
        _prime_base = ask(Q.prime(expr.base), assumptions)
        if _prime_base is False:  
            return False  
        is_exp_not_one = ask(~Q.eq(expr.exp, 1), assumptions)
        if is_exp_not_one:
            return False 
        if _prime_base is True and is_exp_not_one is False:
            return True  
```
I have used is_exp_not_one as a separate variable to avoid calling the check for the exponent to be 1 again when we are checking conditions to return ```True```.

