Can you change it to what is being used in the other classes:
```python
    @force.setter
    def force(self, force):
        raise AttributeError('Can\'t set computed attribute `force`.')
```

The general idea of the test looks good to me.
A few possible changes are:
- Making the docstring shorter, just mentioning that the system is a block slipping on a surface is good enough. Optionally, you can mention that you test it for both a positive and negative velocity case.
- Can you make separate tests for the various extensions? Something like:
  ```python
  class TestCoulombKineticFriction:
      @pytest.fixture
      def block_on_surface(self):
          """Short description of the system."""
  
      @pytest.mark.usefixtures("block_on_surface")
      def test_block_on_surface_default(self):
          friction = CoulombKineticFriction(
              self.mu_k, self.normal_force, self.pathway
          )
          self.system.add_actuators(friction)
          eoms = self.system.form_eoms()
  
          expected_force = self.mu_k * self.normal_force  # Positive case
          assert simplify(
              eoms[0].xreplace({self.u1: 3.141}) -
              (self.F - expected_force - self.m * self.u1.diff())
          ) == 0
          assert simplify(
              eoms[0].xreplace({self.u1: 3.141}) -
              (self.F + expected_force - self.m * self.u1.diff())
          ) == 0
  
      @pytest.mark.usefixtures("block_on_surface")
      def test_block_on_surface_stribeck(self):
          ...
  
      @pytest.mark.usefixtures("block_on_surface")
      def test_block_on_surface_viscous(self):
          ...
  
      @pytest.mark.usefixtures("block_on_surface")
      def test_block_on_surface_all(self):
          ...
  ```

Either use `\mu_k f_n` and `\mu_s f_n` directly or explicity define :math:`f_c=\mu_k f_n` in the explanation (similarly for `f_{max}`). My personal preference would be the first, as it only appears one in the expression:
```suggestion
        F = (\mu_k f_n + (\mu_s - \mu_k) f_n e^{-(\frac{v}{v_s})^2}) \text{sign}(v) + \sigma  v
```
You could even try and see how underbrace looks:
```
        F = \underbrace{\mu_k f_n \text{sign}(v)}_\text{default term}
        + \underbrace{(\mu_s - \mu_k) f_n e^{-(\frac{v}{v_s})^2} \text{sign}(v)}_\text{Stribeck term}
        + \underbrace{\sigma  v}_\text{viscous term}
```

