review_comment:
If you only specify a getter of a property, trying to set a value will already raise an AttributeError. Therefore, you can remove the setters that only raise an AttributeError, i.e.:
```python
def __init__(self, ...):
    self._linear_stiffness = sympify(linear_stiffness, strict=True)
    ...

@property
def linear_stiffness(self):
    return self._linear_stiffness
```
If you prefer having a separate setter to match the implementation of `LinearSpring`, `LinearDamper`, etc., then I would propose trying to use the same format, i.e.:
```python
def __init__(self, ...):
    self.linear_stiffness = linear_stiffness
    ...

@property
def linear_stiffness(self):
    return self._linear_stiffness

@linear_stiffness.setter
def linear_stiffness(self, linear_stiffness):
        if hasattr(self, '_linear_stiffness'):
            msg = (
                f'Can\'t set attribute `linear_stiffness` to '
                f'{repr(linear_stiffness)} as it is immutable.'
            )
            raise AttributeError(msg)
        self._linear_stiffness = sympify(linear_stiffness, strict=True)
```

