We could change that I guess. The definition of `as_powers_dict` is basically:
```python
def as_powers_dict(expr):
    powers = defaultdict(list)
    for arg in Mul.make_args(expr):
        base, exp = arg.as_base_exp()
        powers[base].append(exp)
    return {base: Add(*exponents) for base, exponents in powers.items()}
```
In the mean time could open an issue with SymEngine.

What I think we likely want here is to make this method private, i.e. `_compute_loads`. And then `ActuatorBase` can implement `to_loads`, which in the case of `ForceActuator` will just have:

```python
class ForceActuator(ActuatorBase):
    ....
    def to_loads(self) -> list[tuple[Point, Vector]]:
        return self.pathway._compute_loads(self.force)
```

@TJStienstra previously did some work looking into different options for calculating the magnitude that result in the absolute expression. He benchmarked a few options and the best candidate is below.

```python
def _magnitude(vector: Vector) -> Expr:
    """Fast magnitude that replaces ``sqrt(<...>**2)`` with ``Abs(<...>)``.

    Notes
    =====

    See https://github.com/sympy/sympy/issues/23173 for discussion about
    simplification of e.g. ``sqrt(q(t)**2)`` to ``Abs(q(t))``.

    """
    out = S.Zero
    for i, v1 in enumerate(vector.args):
        out += (v1[0].T * v1[0])[0]
        for v2 in vector.args[i + 1:]:
            out += 2 * ((v2[0].T) * v2[1].dcm(v1[1]) * (v1[0]))[0]
    if isinstance(out, Pow) and out.args[1] == 2:
        return abs(out.args[0])
    return sqrt(out)
```

