review_comment:
`__init_subclass__` modifies the subclass object (not the instance) when the class is created by `type` (i.e. where the line `class mysubclass(Basic):` is)

i.e.:
```python
class A:
    def __init_subclass__(cls):
        print('foo')

class B(A):
    pass
```

Will print `'foo'` eventhough you never instantiate `B`. I don't think `__init__` can take over what `__init_subclass__` is doing here.

```suggestion

def test_issue__evaluateFalse_24288():
```

